# Grid and layout

The engine, painting, hairlines, and lattice alignment live in
`design-system/grid/engine.css`. These laws govern on top of them.

## The band system

Five anchors where a design exists and renders exact; four structural gates
at the bands' geometric midpoints, gated by **container queries** on
`.site-root` — never media queries.

| band | gate (`@container min-width`) | anchor | tick `--t` |
|---|---|---|---|
| base | — | 384 | 32px |
| `rs` | 470 | 576 | 48px |
| `rt` | 665 | 768 | 64px |
| `rd1` | 860 | 960 | 80px |
| `rd2` | 1130 | 1344 | 112px |

- The tick is the page container's width ÷ 12, capped at 112px. Base CSS is
  the 384 design (mobile-first); each gate overrides upward.
- **Nearest-anchor rendering.** Every width renders the nearest anchor's
  design: stretched below its anchor, compressed above the gate. In a
  compressed slice the weights collapse to a pure zoom of that anchor, so wrap
  counts and designed clearances hold by construction.
- Above 1344 the tick caps, the weights pin at 0/1, and the 1344 design
  renders byte-exact at every wider width. The page box (12t) centers on a
  whole-pixel offset (`--page-x`); the freed gutters paint the side fields
  (`.gf-*`, deterministic server-side hash, never animated). Fixed chrome
  carries `--page-x`.
- Three-anchor pages may be designed at 384 · 768 · 1344 only. `rs` renders
  the 384 design on its tick; `rd1` derives from the 1344 design unless the
  section rules otherwise. A frame drawn later supersedes the derivation.
- JavaScript that needs the band measures the container (ResizeObserver) or
  reads computed style — never `matchMedia`.
- Design and acceptance cover all five anchors and one width per structural
  slice, scrollbar forced on. The automated grid sweep is only for lattice
  contract changes — see `00-workflow.mdc`.

## The four units

Every dimension belongs to exactly one unit. Classifying it is the first
decision.

1. **Ticks** — structure: column positions, section heights, exposure regions,
   component footprints. Written in `--t`; fluid by construction.
2. **Weights** — text columns: wrap-pinning widths and text-block heights in
   flow margins interpolate `calc(var(--wA)*V0 + var(--wB)*V1)` between the
   band's anchors.
3. **Em at anchor ratios** — line-internal spacing: inline gaps, chip paddings
   ride the type.
4. **Material** — fixed px per band: buttons, icons, marks, control inners.
   Primitives are material (fixed px per size variant, never band-aware);
   sections choose the size variant per band and own each instance's width.

A px value that secretly encodes ticks, weights, or type is a defect: it breaks
rag, fit, or clearance in the compressed slices. Constants that reference `--t`
or the weights are declared on `.page`, never `:root`.

## The grid and type laws

- **Hold-then-switch.** Type interpolates between a band's two anchors only
  when both carry the same structure. At a structural switch, type holds its
  last designed value across the band and switches with the structure at the
  gate.
- **Size and line-height interpolate; weight and tracking are band
  constants**, restated per band.
- **Band constants ride the weights.** A designed value constant across a band
  is `calc((var(--wA) + var(--wB)) * V)`, never `Vpx`, so it zooms with the
  tick in the compressed slices. Fixed px broke wraps and rail clearance the
  day sections met the compressed slices.
- **Line-inclusive content boxes.** A bordered box whose edge lies on exposed
  lattice is sized `k·t + 1px`, so its border lands on the line's pixel. A box
  sized `k·t` exactly doubles the hairline.
- **The tick wins.** A whole-tick box height governs over its declared padding
  sum; slack compresses padding or sits as clear space.
- **Content clears the lattice.** Flowed content never touches a tick line or
  another block and keeps at least half a tick of vertical clearance from the
  next section or a bottom-anchored block. Copy never silently compresses a
  gap and never overlaps a sibling: when it needs more room the frame grows
  (see *Flow frames*). **The painted lattice rides the growth**: a region
  drawn against content anchors to the edge it belongs to (`gy` from the
  section top, `gyb` from the section bottom, both to stretch between them),
  so it moves with the frame. A cell left on its drawn row while content
  moves is a defect.
- **Derived states.** When design supplies one archetype state, siblings
  derive as the smallest whole-tick height that fits their content at the
  band's internals; the derivation is written in a comment at the value.
- **Choreographies settle.** A load choreography ends explicitly on the final
  beat's `animationend`; band-gated variants re-entering the tree have nothing
  to replay.
- **Proportional primitives.** A primitive may be declared proportional (the
  pricing button): every interior value is a designed-px-over-anchor-cell
  fraction, so a mount that passes the page tick scales the control with the
  grid. The tell in Figma: when a set's cells equal the anchor ticks
  (32/48/64/80/112), read those px as `1t`.
- **Overlay chrome** (the nav) never participates in a section's tick stack.
- **Audits at rest.** Mid-flight motion may be fractional-tick by design; the
  stack-sum, landmark, and exposed-cell clearance audits assert at rest in
  every state. A clearance failure is a collision first.
- **Rendered truth over metadata.** Every landmark is verified against
  rendered bounds, never Figma metadata `x`/`y`.

## Flow frames

A design fixes every block's rows at its anchor; a render between anchors,
or with longer copy, must not collide. Every section is therefore a **flow
frame**: the designed rows are minimums, and content that needs more room
pushes everything after it down by exactly what it needs.

- A section, card, or text block declares its designed rows as
  `min-height: calc(var(--t) * k)`, never `height`. Media frames and
  controls that hold a fixed tick footprint (`k·t` images, buttons, the
  system diagram) are **tick blocks** and keep `height`.
- A text block that owns rows uses `.flow-budget` with `--budget` set to
  those rows; its trailing half tick is the clearance to the next block and
  is slack inside the budget.
- Blocks stack in normal flow: CSS grid (`grid-template-areas`, one row per
  designed row band, `minmax(k·t, auto)` rows) or a flex column. A block's
  designed offset from the previous block is `margin-top`; a bottom-anchored
  block in a frame is `margin-top: auto`, not `position: absolute; bottom`.
  A shared-pixel seam is `margin-bottom: -1px` on the flowed box.
- Text wraps inside `max-width`, never a fixed `width`.
- A gap derived by subtraction (a designed top minus the block above, a
  tick minus a material row) floors with `max()` at a spacing token. Type
  stops zooming below the smallest anchor while the tick keeps shrinking, so
  an unfloored derivation goes negative and blocks collide.
- **The flow contract is what the grid checks verify.** At an anchor every
  section renders its designed rows exactly — growth at an anchor is a defect
  in the section. Between anchors a section may grow by what its copy needs;
  every later section moves down by that growth, and the stack sum carries
  it. Landmarks inside a grown section are audited at the anchors.
- Regions anchor to the section edge they belong to (`gy`, `gyb`, or both to
  stretch); nothing publishes per-content "extra ticks".

## Lattice paint and photography

Painting the lattice and placing content are one decision, made per section
band before any region map is written. Three states, a closed set:

- **unpainted** — objects follow type and `.gx` carries no content fields; at
  most a one-row closer at the section bottom. The blog post is the model,
  and its silence at `rd` is a format constraint (the sticky TOC rail), not a
  default: a static marketing page paints at every band.
- **bookend** — the composition is punctuation around photography that must
  not wear hairlines. Paint stops at least one full tick before the object,
  the object renders in unpainted air, and paint resumes at least one full
  tick after (a closer row with a dedicated empty tick above it satisfies
  the resume). Inside the unpainted field the object may use any unit —
  `1fr` cells, aspect-ratio boxes, a tick slab after flowing type.
- **seated** — objects are the grid: every cell solves in whole or half
  ticks on its content column (`n·c + (n−1)·g`), cells have tick origins,
  and the paint is exactly the rectangles the cells occupy.

The test is mechanical: overlay the lattice; every painted line either
coincides with a content box edge or crosses no photography. A field painted
through off-grid media is a defect in every state. Each section file
declares its state in a comment above its region map:
`/* Lattice: bookend — paint band under the head, wall unpainted, closer. */`

On a painted static page the last in-flow object keeps at least one full
tick of empty rows before the next paint or the section end; a hug budget
(the ceiling of measured content) does not satisfy this — the clearance
tick is designed into the frame, never residual.

## Layout must scale — no fragile positioning

Absolute positioning is acceptable only for: media fills (`inset: 0;
object-fit: cover` in a grid-sized parent); the exposure vocabulary
(`--gx`/`--gy`/`--gyb` ticks); overlay layers (nav chrome, modal backdrops,
decorative pseudo-elements, a menu anchored to its control, a tag hanging off
a card edge); a true escape from document flow (viewport-fixed controls, the
case-study rail). Everything else is normal flow.

| Anti-pattern | Fix |
|---|---|
| Fixed px coordinates from Figma's ruler | ticks/weights, grid areas or flex order + `margin-top` |
| `position: absolute; top: k·t` on ordinary content | grid row / flow order with a tick `margin-top` |
| `height: k·t` on a section or text frame | `min-height: k·t` (flow frame) |
| Fixed px widths on text containers | `max-width` weight-riding wrap boxes |
| Per-content height overrides (`extraTicks`, breakpoint-specific `height`) | flow frame that grows with its content |
| `line-height: 0` as a spacing hack | flex column + gap |
| Magic gaps | tick or em expressions per the four units |
| Media queries for band switches | container queries at the gates |

Always: no fixed widths that overflow at 384; touch targets ≥ 44 × 44 px;
no hover-only interactions.
